WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.960
[Music]
Toastmasters and guests, it is my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:17.280
pleasure to welcome International President-Elect&nbsp;
Distinguished Toastmaster Margaret Page.&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.560
-Welcome to the 2021 World Championship of Public&nbsp;
Speaking Showcase. My name is Margaret Page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:33.120
and I am Toastmasters International&nbsp;
President-Elect and will be your emcee for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:43.120
showcase. Congratulations to all three contestants&nbsp;
who share this global virtual stage of the World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:54.240
Championship of Public Speaking. Members and&nbsp;
guests, meet the 2021 World Championship winners:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:08.080
World Champion Verity Price, second place winner&nbsp;
Nitay-Yair Levi, and our third-place winner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:17.120
Roger Caesar. During today's showcase we are&nbsp;
going to speak with each winner for a few minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:28.160
But first I invite our new World Champion Verity&nbsp;
to enjoy two minutes to share acknowledgements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:35.680
with our audience. Over to you, Verity.
-Thank you so much, Margaret. I-I must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:44.720
say two minutes to say what I have to say would&nbsp;
take a lifetime, but I have 120 seconds so I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:50.800
start with the two most important words which are&nbsp;
thank you. I’m so humbled and happy and grateful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.600
to have received this title today, I’m going&nbsp;
to be pinching myself for weeks to come.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.200
Thank you to my club Toasted Toastmasters,&nbsp;
to my incredible District 74 Southern Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.880
who have supported me and sent me messages saying&nbsp;
it's time for Africa—and today we discovered that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:12.960
it was. I am blown away to be the first winner&nbsp;
of this title in Africa, I hope that I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.480
broken the proverbial four-minute mile for&nbsp;
all speakers on this incredible continent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.120
because I really believe that this title can&nbsp;
come back to Africa many, many times. Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.160
you to my husband who has looked after our&nbsp;
little boy so I could stay in the bedroom and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.200
deliver speeches at all the incredible clubs that&nbsp;
have opened their doors to me across the world;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.200
it's been the most extraordinary journey. I want&nbsp;
to also give a big shout out to Pres Vasilev for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.400
his amazing compelling storytelling course that&nbsp;
I bought at the start of my journey and used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.840
to help me craft my final speech. And then my&nbsp;
really big shout out to my coach, Lance Miller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.360
My favorite World Champ of all time, and to have&nbsp;
had the privilege of have you coach me, Lance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:03.120
and bring me to this point, I’m absolutely blown&nbsp;
away. It's been extraordinary. But mostly to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.880
to Toastmasters International, to the audiences&nbsp;
that have made this possible for all of us, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:15.440
to my fellow contestants to share this stage with&nbsp;
you, I know only three of us could be at the top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.720
but all eight speeches today blew me away. To my&nbsp;
semi-finalists, to all the dreams that were born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.720
in this contest and they're gonna come back to the&nbsp;
stage next year, I can't wait to-to see what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:33.600
bring. But if I can leave you with nothing else,&nbsp;
it's that right now I’m sitting in a room similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:39.440
to one that for many years felt like a prison.&nbsp;
And today it's shown me that a prison can turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:45.360
into a platform. And coming from South Africa&nbsp;
where our biggest inspiration, Nelson Mandela,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.960
took imprisonment and turned it into a platform&nbsp;
for change, I believe that as speakers and as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.560
leaders we can all do that. So, thank you from&nbsp;
the bottom of my heart, I am so grateful to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.440
be your world champion and I’m going to be&nbsp;
punching myself for days to come. Thank you.&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.680
-Thank you, Verity, for your gracious&nbsp;
and inspiring words. You have thanked a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:16.000
lot of people along your personal journey who&nbsp;
have influenced your success up to this very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:23.600
moment. These acknowledgments are indicators of&nbsp;
a true Toastmaster leader, congratulations again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.320
Later in the showcase we will ask the winners&nbsp;
questions submitted by those of you watching us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:36.480
live today. If you would like to submit a&nbsp;
question, please use the Q&amp;A feature on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:43.680
platform, type in your question and hit send.&nbsp;
We will do our very best to get to as many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:49.920
questions as possible. But please be aware, we&nbsp;
may not have time to get to all your questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:58.320
So first, I have a few questions for our winners.&nbsp;
I’d like to go back to Verity again and ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.920
what does it feel like to&nbsp;
be the 2021 World Champion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:10.080
of Public Speaking? Take us through the&nbsp;
range of emotions you have experienced in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:15.920
the last few hours since you were declared&nbsp;
the World Champion of Public Speaking.&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.920
-Margaret, I think I will feel what it feels&nbsp;
like—tomorrow when I wake up—at the moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:30.240
I honestly can say I feel like I’m in a bubble,&nbsp;
but I was so privileged I got to watch with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.280
whole lot of family and friends the first time in&nbsp;
a long time that I had quite a few people with me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.240
And when it was announced the joy around me&nbsp;
of the people that believe in me and support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:46.480
me…I just felt like it flooded through my whole&nbsp;
body, and we danced, and we sang, and we just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:54.720
celebrated. We felt like we'd won a world cup.&nbsp;
And I just feel so grateful, and I think-I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.640
for me right now what's-what's settling in is that&nbsp;
the last six months and everything I’ve learned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:04.960
now it's time for me to figure out how&nbsp;
I share that and how I give that back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:09.520
especially into my District and into my fellow&nbsp;
members. Because I’ve got lots that I can share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:14.800
now to make this journey easier for the ones&nbsp;
who come behind me. But I’m just over the moon.&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:21.840
-And we can see that and feel that for&nbsp;
you. But many people would like to be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.760
your shoes right now and share what that&nbsp;
immediate onrush of attention feels like.&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:35.840
-Also overwhelming, I haven't had a chance to get&nbsp;
to all the messages. And they've been flooding in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.480
throughout this journey from—and I’m sure all my&nbsp;
fellow contestants have had this since winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:45.280
Districts. You just have people that believe in&nbsp;
you, that reach out that take the time to send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:52.480
messages on social media platforms or WhatsApp,&nbsp;
and I have felt so connected and I think the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:58.160
thing for me that's come through this journey&nbsp;
is, alone I can't, together we can. And I’m sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.840
all my contestants will agree, none of us have&nbsp;
given a speech that we wrote by ourselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.960
It's from the feedback that we've received, that&nbsp;
we've implemented, we've changed, we've grown, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:12.240
I can't wait to get to all the messages and&nbsp;
reply to everyone, but wow. Thank you. Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:18.000
you for everything that's flooding in.
-Indeed. Together we can, right? Yeah.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:26.800
So, Nitay-Yair, I’d like to go to you. How does&nbsp;
it feel to be the second-place winner of the ‘21&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:33.920
World Championship of Public Speaking? Um, take&nbsp;
us through the range of emotions you have felt.&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.520
-I-I don't-I don't still-I don't think I even&nbsp;
realized that here, you know, the-the magnitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.520
of that achievement. Uh, as Verity said, there's&nbsp;
a lot of work that goes straight, there's a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:50.240
of people that help you, and-and just a moment in&nbsp;
time of something that had been happening for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:54.560
very long time and there's a lot of people&nbsp;
I need to thank, uh, for that moment. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:58.640
I’ll echo Verity: I think only&nbsp;
tomorrow that will settle in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:04.640
and only tomorrow I’ll realize what has been&nbsp;
achieved, and time to get ready for next year.&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:13.840
-Thank you, yes, indeed, tomorrow. Nitay-Yair,&nbsp;
what lasting impression do you hope your speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.480
made on the audience? If members&nbsp;
are talking about your speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:23.200
one year from now, what one thing&nbsp;
would you like them to be saying?&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:31.600
-In terms of the final speech is my message,&nbsp;
which perhaps sounds simple, but very hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:37.200
do is to show love to those who are important to&nbsp;
you. Because they're there the whole time and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.600
take them sometime for granted. I have throughout&nbsp;
my past, especially with my mother which I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:48.000
very much. And it's very easy to-to take people&nbsp;
for granted. But they really shouldn't be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:55.040
because they love to use what propels you forward.&nbsp;
So, always show your love. Simple as that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:09:02.960
And as hard as it says-as it may not be to do.
-Thank you for those beautiful words.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:11.040
Roger, how does it feel to be there on this big&nbsp;
virtual platform as the third-place winner of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:19.600
the ‘21 World Championship of Public Speaking?
-It feels amazing. Absolutely amazing. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.240
surreal, and I-I echo what&nbsp;
my fellow contestants said.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:32.720
You-you go through all this process, the amount of&nbsp;
work, the-the coaching, the feedback that you get,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.000
the support from all those around you, I&nbsp;
mean…you go through the entire process and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.680
all you hope for is a chance. And that's what&nbsp;
it comes down to—a chance to get here so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:50.160
you could spread your message and be able to&nbsp;
inspire others. I mean for me in my message was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:56.320
that—to inspire others to find their greatness&nbsp;
and I think by looking at all of these people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:03.440
Verity and Nitay-Yair and the other competitors&nbsp;
today…that's what they were doing: trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:11.040
inspire our audience in our own way through our&nbsp;
stories, and make sure that they also can find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.000
their greatness in some way, shape or form. So, I&nbsp;
am I’m thrilled, absolutely thrilled to be here.&nbsp;

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:22.400
-Well, we-we certainly found your&nbsp;
greatness. Roger, what inspired you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:28.720
to write and speak about what has now become&nbsp;
your winning speech, entitled, “Is it in you?”&nbsp;

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:37.200
-Well, I’ll take you back to the speech that I&nbsp;
did in the semi-finals and…my father and I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:43.200
had this relationship where there are moments when&nbsp;
I was just cringing about him, and then there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:50.160
moments when I realized, “Wow he's such a great&nbsp;
man.” And my mom has always been that verbal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:55.040
influence in my life, and I felt&nbsp;
that it was important that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:00.080
bring the two together and show how&nbsp;
much they have meant to me. My parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:06.080
mean the world to me. My sister, yeah, she&nbsp;
means something to me too…she will not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:11.360
singing anywhere, and that is okay with me,&nbsp;
but she means a lot to me. But my parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:18.480
they have done so much hard work throughout the&nbsp;
years and the lessons that I have learned along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:25.120
the way have been so instrumental in who I am&nbsp;
today and I’m proud. And that's why I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:31.600
people to understand that sometimes we don't see&nbsp;
those who are inspiring us, and they're right in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:39.680
front of us. They're right there. Uh, Verity had&nbsp;
her dad, you know, uh, Nitay-Yair had his mom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:44.720
These are people that we don't see what they're&nbsp;
actually doing for us, and then all of a sudden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:50.400
it [Snaps] clicks. That they're important to us.&nbsp;
So, this is what I wanted people to understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:55.840
how valuable those people are around us. And-and&nbsp;
I have so many people to thank along the way.&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:04.880
-Indeed, those families mean so much to us and&nbsp;
teach us so much about life. Here's a question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:12.640
that I’d love for all three of you to answer. How&nbsp;
did you modify your speech for a: your virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:18.800
audience, and b: for your international&nbsp;
audience? And we'll go to Verity first.&nbsp;

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:29.360
-Thank you, thank you, Margaret. A lot of my&nbsp;
modifications happened from practicing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:35.360
clubs around the world and it was so helpful&nbsp;
for me because there was things in my speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:40.240
like we lived in what we call a caravan, but when&nbsp;
I practiced in the United States they were like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:45.520
“We would call it a campervan,” and then I could&nbsp;
just tweak and change words slightly which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:51.280
so helpful. And in terms of the virtual&nbsp;
space, I think where we're lucky is we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:56.800
had a year-and-a-half of getting used to doing&nbsp;
this virtually. I was so inspired watching last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:02.160
year's contest and particularly Mike Carr's&nbsp;
winning speech and how he used the screen. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:09.360
you know, he and his fellow contestants paved the&nbsp;
way for me to play in this format. So, that for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:14.880
wasn't the difficult part. What was-was exciting&nbsp;
for me was getting the feedback from audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:23.680
in Bangladesh, in India, Australia, UK, America,&nbsp;
and just finding language and imagery that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:29.440
as broad a universal connection. Otherwise,&nbsp;
if I’m only getting feedback in South Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:35.360
we often don't realize that we're just coming from&nbsp;
one point of view. Without this virtual world I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:39.840
don't know if I would have been able to make it&nbsp;
as accessible as I feel like I did in the end.&nbsp;

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:50.000
-It was a real advantage this year wasn't it? And&nbsp;
yes, you are truly all pioneers here. Nitay-Yair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:56.960
how did you modify your speech for a: your virtual&nbsp;
audience and b: your international audience?&nbsp;

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:04.080
-This is a very, very interesting question,&nbsp;
Margaret, and I’ll answer it in two levels:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:10.320
I think…for me this is about creating art. It's&nbsp;
called the art of public speaking, not public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:17.680
speaking. And when you look at it as art you&nbsp;
need to go outside of the box and-and-and go as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:22.720
crazy as you can, and then turn it down based on&nbsp;
feedback. And I had great, uh, coaches this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:28.720
Pres Vasilev coached me this year and I thank you&nbsp;
very much. And Lou Beaton, who's listening I hope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:32.000
thank you as well. And the reason I&nbsp;
mentioned that is because you take these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:38.000
crazy ideas and then they help you tone it&nbsp;
down to what's maybe palatable to the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:42.880
as opposed to scare the audience. Some of the&nbsp;
stuff I wanted to do perhaps were too extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:47.920
for the virtual, uh, sense, but the feedback that&nbsp;
you get is great. So, that's number one you need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:54.160
that circle of confidence, uh, and obviously&nbsp;
Bill and-and Sherry helped on that too. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:59.040
other thing is the clubs visits and-and the&nbsp;
greatest advantage—I’m a fairly new toastmaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:04.800
I’m only two years and a big toastmaster and this&nbsp;
year people like Keith Hardy and Janet open and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:10.400
Alicia as well open the door for me, because I&nbsp;
don't know how many clubs, to visit over I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:16.400
know 70 clubs around the world. And when you test&nbsp;
the messages, especially in my semi-final speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:23.600
in-in Africa, or in Vietnam, or in Singapore,&nbsp;
or in the United States and I think Verity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:27.920
touched on a very important point: your message&nbsp;
needs to be global. It needs to be simple and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:32.880
global. You can't use terms that people won't&nbsp;
understand in different countries, and I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:38.560
originally at the beginning, so the feedback is&nbsp;
extremely important. A simple message, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:46.880
key to me is to focus on your message and deliver&nbsp;
it in an artful way. If I can summarize it I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:55.120
focus on the art. But support your message, yeah.
-I love-I love that phrasing that public speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.120 --> 00:16:02.080
is an art, and that is truly, absolutely&nbsp;
correct. Roger, it's your turn now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:10.640
How did you modify your speech for your virtual&nbsp;
audience and for your international audience?&nbsp;

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:16.640
-Well for my final speech I had&nbsp;
created that speech a few years ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:24.720
and when I was, again as many of you have said&nbsp;
already, I worked with Pres Vasilev as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:34.720
in the first part of it in trying to create it&nbsp;
and-and have it for the in-person audience. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:40.000
I remember once we realized of course now that&nbsp;
we were going to be doing it online, there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:44.160
lot of things that you have to change. I mean some&nbsp;
of the things that you would have in your speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:51.440
and I’m sure that, uh, many of my other fellow&nbsp;
contestants can say the same thing, is that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:55.120
may have a moment where you're connecting with an&nbsp;
audience and you're looking one-on-one with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.120 --> 00:17:00.000
well now you're only looking at this little screen&nbsp;
right here, and you're trying so hard to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:06.480
that you can still bring your message through, and&nbsp;
they would feel it on the other side of that lens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:10.960
And that was what we had-I had to change when&nbsp;
I came to-when it came to the speech. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:18.800
I worked with Craig Valentine this year and&nbsp;
he was instrumental in helping me to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:25.760
the funny parts, the emotional parts, how to bring&nbsp;
it so that it can connect. But most importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:30.960
two people that I must-must thank and that&nbsp;
is Catherine McKenzie, who is the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:36.880
coach ever, and also Neil Dunsmore, who is a&nbsp;
great friend of mine, and we worked tirelessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:42.320
to make sure that we were able to work through&nbsp;
this little screen here and make it work. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:51.520
with regards to the global side of it, I will&nbsp;
say this: our stories are a gateway to our souls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:59.040
and it's the pathway to the audience's heart.&nbsp;
And what we try to do is make sure that when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:05.760
at least for myself, whatever message that I’m&nbsp;
sending out there, it is going to be universal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:12.480
You know, our stories are personal, but our&nbsp;
message has to be universal. And that was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:19.440
key to do something that, yes, could be personal&nbsp;
in my story, but the message itself everybody can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:26.720
take away from, and that was important to me.
-We did definitely feel all your-all eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:32.400
speeches, they were just so remarkable.&nbsp;
And definitely, you countered our souls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:39.040
with that-that, uh, entry point there. So, I&nbsp;
have another question for all three of you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:47.280
How has Toastmasters helped you personally and&nbsp;
professionally? But this time we will start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:52.560
Nitay-Yair. Would you like to&nbsp;
answer the first time-for the first-&nbsp;

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:57.200
-Oh, God. This is-I’m going to embarrass&nbsp;
myself now, but heck with it, let's do it. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:04.800
when I got married to my wife, Simone, who I my-my&nbsp;
nickname for her is “Bundle of love with big brown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:09.280
eyes,” that's what the name big brown eyes come&nbsp;
from, that's my nickname for her, and that's why I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:15.280
named all the other characters based on the eyes.&nbsp;
And we got married and she's not a toastmaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:20.400
I wasn't a toastmaster at the time, and she did&nbsp;
this speech in our wedding, and she killed it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:24.880
She would have beat everyone today on the&nbsp;
speech. I’m biased by the way, sorry, Verity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:31.920
So, the-the-the-and then I did my speech, and&nbsp;
it was a disaster. It was an absolutely bomb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:37.520
in my own wedding and I thought, “Okay, that&nbsp;
needs to change. That is that is not good.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:43.440
Uh, and I was very—believed or not, very shy.&nbsp;
I’m still very shy, and-and I don't necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:49.840
feel comfortable speaking in front of a large&nbsp;
audience, so the Toastmaster-Toastmasters journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:54.480
I joined this club in the City Tattersalls&nbsp;
club—which I represent today—and it took me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.480 --> 00:20:00.400
six months to do my icebreaker. I was that afraid.&nbsp;
Six months to do my icebreaker. But once I did my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:07.840
icebreaker, uh, my mentor Mike Keanu MacCallum and&nbsp;
Peter Anderson as well immediately came and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:14.800
uh, “You need to do the International Speech&nbsp;
Contest.” …on the same year! And-and what it did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:20.000
it-it-what Toastmaster's environment or the&nbsp;
mentors that I have in Toastmasters did, they gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:24.400
me confidence. And the thing is you have a skill&nbsp;
set but until you have the confidence to use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:29.440
skill set, it's just a skill set that is buried&nbsp;
somewhere underneath the ground. So, what it did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:35.520
it elevated the confidence. But beyond that, it&nbsp;
created a network of supporters and people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:40.560
help you push toward your dream going forward.&nbsp;
And that is something that you-just doesn't exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:45.120
anywhere else. People you don't even-there's&nbsp;
some people who help me who I haven't even met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:49.760
physically. Who I haven't even met, and&nbsp;
they live in the US, I live in Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:56.000
and-and I just-it's incredible. So, how did it&nbsp;
help me? Confidence. Absolutely confidence. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:00.080
know, we all have something burning inside,&nbsp;
the question is how do you bring it out?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:06.080
For me that was Toastmasters.
-That was a beautiful story beautiful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:11.600
beautiful story and I hope we will hear the&nbsp;
speech about you and your wife, uh, a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:17.360
five-to-seven minutes and not-on another time but&nbsp;
thank you for that. And the Toastmaster family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:24.320
is definitely remarkable we'll-we are a unique&nbsp;
organization; only things like that happen here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:31.920
Roger, your turn. How has Toastmasters&nbsp;
helped you personally and professionally?&nbsp;

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:40.720
-Well, I’ll share my story. When I took over the&nbsp;
family business I used to walk into a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:46.480
companies that we work with and they knew me while&nbsp;
my dad was around as just ‘hey it's Roger, how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:51.920
doing, Roger? What's going on?’ And when I took&nbsp;
over the business, you saw the change in how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:59.360
reacted to me and it was more like, ‘oh, here's&nbsp;
the owner so let's, uh, let's see what he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.360 --> 00:22:06.880
about.’ And I remember sitting in an executive&nbsp;
office one day and he had just become the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:14.800
supervisor of the company that we work with,&nbsp;
and it was the first time ever I encountered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:21.760
trying to speak to an introvert, and I had&nbsp;
no idea how to have a conversation with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:25.920
and I remember leaving his office and coming&nbsp;
back to mind and saying some words that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:31.120
can't say because we're Toastmasters. I&nbsp;
couldn't say it and of course I looked up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:37.840
how to improve my speaking abilities,&nbsp;
and that's how I got into Toastmasters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:43.520
And I’ll never forget my first speech that I did&nbsp;
when I did my icebreaker. There was a gentleman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:48.480
there named Sanjay Sharma and once I finished&nbsp;
he came over he tapped my shoulders, and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:53.840
everybody has one of these stories…I’m hoping.&nbsp;
But he tapped my shoulder after, and he goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.400 --> 00:23:00.000
“You're gonna be a champion one day, I can&nbsp;
feel it. I know it, I know it in my soul,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:07.440
and I laughed at him. Well, I’m not laughing&nbsp;
anymore, thank you, Sanjay, for that. So, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:12.320
where it is for me. Personally and professionally,&nbsp;
Toastmasters has helped me build my confidence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:17.360
be able to feel comfortable speaking, whether&nbsp;
in person or right now on the virtual stage.&nbsp;

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:24.800
-Thank you for sharing that story too, very&nbsp;
special and precious—I guarantee no one said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:32.400
that to me, “you will be champion,” so, they only&nbsp;
say it to those that are-have it within them. Well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:39.280
done, Roger. Verity, how has Toastmasters&nbsp;
helped you personally and professionally?&nbsp;

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:46.880
-I joined Toastmasters for fun 10 years ago.&nbsp;
My sister had joined it to overcome her fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:52.640
of public speaking and I used to go to meetings&nbsp;
just to support at Two Oceans Toastmasters and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:56.960
loved it. I would do table topics and I thought it&nbsp;
was great. I was a professional singer and already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.960 --> 00:24:01.360
speaking at the time, so it was really like&nbsp;
this is something interesting for me to do once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:07.920
a month. But I was also moments away from living&nbsp;
in the spare room and suffering from quite severe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:15.520
depression and anxiety. And so, how Toastmasters&nbsp;
helped me was personally, it put me into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:20.160
organization where suddenly I had a purpose bigger&nbsp;
than myself. I was invited to join the committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:25.920
and I joined as a treasurer and someone who did&nbsp;
very bad at maths, I couldn't believe they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:31.120
trusting me with the books. But suddenly I was&nbsp;
part of a team, and I actually joined a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:35.680
club so that I would have a reason to get up on&nbsp;
a Wednesday morning and go to a meeting. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:42.000
really suffering from bad depression at the time.&nbsp;
And it was a conscious choice, this organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:47.680
is going to help me. There's people here, there's&nbsp;
community, there's a sense of camaraderie. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:55.680
I kept showing up…and I had to overcome my&nbsp;
arrogance that I was already sorted as a speaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.680 --> 00:25:00.960
because my sister, Kay, in the club contest in&nbsp;
2012 beat me and I was very unimpressed because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:07.120
she'd only done a cc6 and she went on to come&nbsp;
second to Ryan Avery in the semi-finals that year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:13.040
And I suddenly learned that you need to read&nbsp;
the manual and there is always something you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:17.120
could learn, and you have to be mentored, and&nbsp;
you can't think you know everything. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:23.360
I’m only here today because I’ve spent nine years&nbsp;
listening in this organization, taking feedback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:28.000
and leaning into the leadership opportunities it's&nbsp;
given me, because that's where it's really changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:32.960
me. It's changed the way I run my business, after&nbsp;
being treasurer, I’ve never struggled to get money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:40.560
out of a client again. It's been amazing, so it's&nbsp;
been life-changing and I might gloss over the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:45.600
that I was in a spare room eating ice cream and&nbsp;
Netflix, but it was a very dark time in my life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:50.240
and this organization gave me purpose&nbsp;
through that. So, I will be forever grateful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.240 --> 00:25:53.200
and forever looking for ways to&nbsp;
give back to this organization.&nbsp;

00:25:55.040 --> 00:26:02.960
-We're so thankful that you got over the ice&nbsp;
cream and the, uh, Netflix binging for sure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:10.560
And thank you for that about reading the manual,&nbsp;
now the project, right? You-you have to read-yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.560 --> 00:26:15.600
do the work. Do the work together. Thank you&nbsp;
all for those answers, they were beautiful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.240 --> 00:26:22.640
But right now, we want to hear from Verity on how&nbsp;
many times have you presented that speech you gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:30.640
during the World Championship of Public Speaking,&nbsp;
and what did your coaches or mentors say or do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:37.840
that made the difference in your decision&nbsp;
to pursue this World Champion title?&nbsp;

00:26:40.160 --> 00:26:47.600
-So, the speech I gave today I wrote exactly 100&nbsp;
days ago. And the reason I know that is it came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:53.280
me at two in the morning and the original version,&nbsp;
although very similar to the story I told today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:59.120
was also sharing that I had done one of the things&nbsp;
I did based on my father's advice was a 100 happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:03.840
days challenge—to just take pictures of things&nbsp;
that made me happy. And so, when I wrote the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:07.600
speech I thought, “you know what?” And I looked&nbsp;
at the date, and I was like, “Gosh, it's exactly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:12.640
hundred days till the world finals and if this was&nbsp;
the speech I would give, I’m gonna do my hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:17.680
happy days while I give the speech.” And really,&nbsp;
today has been the happiest day of my life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:25.280
But I’ve given the speech hundreds of times.&nbsp;
It's changed its message multiple times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.280 --> 00:27:31.200
and through working with Lawrence Miller as my-my&nbsp;
coach and he just gently pushed me and guided me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.840 --> 00:27:36.960
he didn't ever kind of push me into directions I&nbsp;
didn't want to go in, but he held the space for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:43.520
to question decisions I was making, try different&nbsp;
things. And through the feedback I had, we only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:49.840
ended up—I’d write a different story two weeks ago&nbsp;
and I actually would like to—so, the same the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:55.760
speech, just the thread came through and that was&nbsp;
feedback, uh, from a toastmaster in South Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.480 --> 00:28:03.040
and he just he made one comment in his evaluation&nbsp;
to me, and it made me re-look at the whole speech.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:10.000
And suddenly it just came together. So, I will be&nbsp;
forever grateful. And that is why I say to anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:16.960
if you choose to go on this journey, deliver your&nbsp;
speech to as many people as you can. Suck it up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:23.520
take the feedback, it's not always easy; my ego&nbsp;
has been bruised and some of it's been brutal, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:28.160
I’ve had to listen. And go, “Is there something,&nbsp;
and if three people have said the same thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:34.000
am I open to change?” So, I have been giving&nbsp;
this speech for 100 days, it has changed me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:40.320
it has shaped me, it has challenged me. But I&nbsp;
would never -would never do it any other way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:45.920
other than rewriting and rewriting and&nbsp;
redelivering and tweaking and changing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:50.080
up until the day of the finals there was little&nbsp;
moments that were still coming to me. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:58.000
I’m sure there's 10 other versions I could give&nbsp;
but I’m very grateful that I’ve now done it.&nbsp;

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:04.160
-Well, that was outstanding to hear how many&nbsp;
times it takes, and really, how long you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.160 --> 00:29:11.680
to work at something till that ultimate idea,&nbsp;
that concept, that really getting it within you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:11.680 --> 00:29:19.120
how long that can sometimes take. So, for all of&nbsp;
us, we need to keep working, keep working. We have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:26.240
so many questions from the audience. We're going&nbsp;
to go to audience questions now. So, the first one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:37.040
is, “There is a thin line between being&nbsp;
vulnerable and over sharing. How do each of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:44.000
navigate this fine line?” So, let's&nbsp;
go to Roger first and then Nitay-Yair.&nbsp;

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:54.800
-That is an absolutely great question. The reality&nbsp;
is-is as speakers the way you connect with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.800 --> 00:30:02.320
audience is being vulnerable. Is allowing them&nbsp;
to see that inner human side of you. I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:08.560
very important to share that. Now the thin line,&nbsp;
yes, you don't want to share too much sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:14.560
because you don't want to expose yourself, but I&nbsp;
kind of look at it from a different perspective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.120 --> 00:30:21.840
And that is that there is somebody in that&nbsp;
audience whether we are in person or virtually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:29.120
there's somebody there that is waiting to hear&nbsp;
a particular message. And sometimes when we as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:38.240
speakers go out there and we speak, it could take&nbsp;
just you opening up yourself and talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.240 --> 00:30:44.640
something that has happened to you that will help&nbsp;
that one person in the audience. And I’ll give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:50.880
a quick example. I went and I spoke once at&nbsp;
an open house and I talked about, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.880 --> 00:30:56.800
fear in an event that had happened to me before&nbsp;
and this gentleman came up to me after the event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:56.800 --> 00:31:00.080
and he said, “You know what, it's funny you&nbsp;
did that. I wasn't going to come here today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.640 --> 00:31:06.080
but somebody said, ‘you know, come and see&nbsp;
this guy.’” And when I finished, he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:14.480
“I was going to go to my lawyers at-after work&nbsp;
at about four o'clock today and sign papers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:19.520
to divorce my wife because I thought she was&nbsp;
the one that was at fault for everything that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:24.480
happened in our relationship.” He said, “After&nbsp;
I heard your speech, I realized it was me that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:29.680
needed to change the things that were going on&nbsp;
with me. And now we're trying to reconcile that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:35.120
we're going to go and reconcile things.” These are&nbsp;
those moments that you as a speaker just go, “Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:41.840
I didn't know I could do that,” but that's&nbsp;
why being vulnerable and allowing yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.840 --> 00:31:48.560
to share your stories—It can help someone else&nbsp;
to cure their own problems that they're having.&nbsp;

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:58.880
-Absolutely. What a gift this tool is, right, to&nbsp;
be able to share with others and have impact on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:07.440
their life and influence them in a positive way.&nbsp;
Thank you for doing that. So, Nitay-Yair, over to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.440 --> 00:32:13.280
you. I’ll read the question again to you from the&nbsp;
audience. “There's a thin line between building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:24.080
vulnerable-” sorry, “-to being vulnerable and over&nbsp;
sharing. How did you navigate this fine line?”&nbsp;

00:32:26.320 --> 00:32:31.200
-The truth is I’m not sure I’m able to navigate&nbsp;
that fine line and that's-that's the absolute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:38.000
truth. Um, my personal view is this: if there's&nbsp;
something, a message that is burning in you, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:43.520
need to get it out. And you need to-to put all the&nbsp;
shields and all the walls that you put around you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:44.240 --> 00:32:49.440
and just share it. And it makes me emotional&nbsp;
now because that's just the truth. And if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:54.480
share your message raw, people will&nbsp;
connect. But you need to be careful;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.480 --> 00:32:58.000
you need to lift them up at the end&nbsp;
of it. You need to open your heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:03.680
say, “This is who I am. You may not like it,&nbsp;
but this is who I am,” and then close it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:03.680 --> 00:33:07.760
take them back up because you-you don't want them&nbsp;
more depressed than-than when they come and saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:14.640
your speech. But unless-unless you go in and show&nbsp;
them the real you, um, I don't-I don't believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:20.240
that there will be any connection. That's my&nbsp;
personal view. I’m not probably the best person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:25.120
to answer that question because I-I don't know&nbsp;
how to separate it too, I really don't. Yeah.&nbsp;

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:31.040
-Thank you, yeah, thank you for that share,&nbsp;
you—and all of you really did lift us up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:36.240
you were marvelous at that. All right,&nbsp;
Verity, I’ll-I’ll read it again. “There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:44.160
a thin line between being vulnerable and over&nbsp;
sharing; how did you navigate this fine line?”&nbsp;

00:33:46.560 --> 00:33:53.200
-Well similar to Nitay-Yair, I’m-I am a very&nbsp;
honest person; my name means truth, so I tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:59.520
to speak about what I’ve dealt with. But I think&nbsp;
the-the line for me is and-and I can't remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:03.920
where I heard this, but they say you should never&nbsp;
speak about something until you can laugh about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:09.440
it. So, even if it was a heartbreaking,&nbsp;
you know, shameful, difficult situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.440 --> 00:34:15.680
don't speak about it until you've dealt with it.&nbsp;
So, I tend to talk about quite difficult things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:15.680 --> 00:34:20.640
that I’ve gone through, and I-I can laugh at&nbsp;
myself and I’m not afraid to share my flaws. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:26.160
less than perfect and-and I talk about&nbsp;
that often, but I’m not using my talk as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.160 --> 00:34:30.880
personal therapy. And maybe that is&nbsp;
where we see something as over sharing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:35.120
is we're using the talk to maybe try&nbsp;
and process it in front of an audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.120 --> 00:34:42.160
whereas the audience as Nitay-Yair said wants&nbsp;
to be lifted up. So, be honest. Be vulnerable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:42.160 --> 00:34:46.560
Open up your arms, you know, own your shame.&nbsp;
People grow and-and Roger said this as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:50.400
we don't know how we might shape someone by&nbsp;
being honest about what we've struggled with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.960 --> 00:34:55.840
But I think for me, I share it once I’ve worked&nbsp;
through it. I couldn't have given this speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.840 --> 00:35:01.360
six years ago. I wasn't ready. I can now laugh&nbsp;
at myself in the spare room and there's times I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:01.360 --> 00:35:07.280
still will eat ice cream and eat-watch Netflix and&nbsp;
that's okay. It's part of my process, but I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:07.280 --> 00:35:14.560
need the audience to heal me, I’m healing myself.
-Thank you for that distinction and that share.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:21.760
So, this message came from the audience, they&nbsp;
wanted to congratulate all three winners and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.760 --> 00:35:28.400
would like to know if this is your first attempt&nbsp;
at the World Championship of Public Speaking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:28.400 --> 00:35:38.800
And we'll let Roger answer that first. Roger?
-Great, great. This is my fourth time. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:47.600
the fourth time I have been here. I-I must admit&nbsp;
I wasn't planning on competing this year and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:53.520
again, my good friend Neil Dunsmore and&nbsp;
I had a conversation and we said, “Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:58.720
let's just go out there and have a&nbsp;
good time and see where it takes us.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:04.400
And so, we both decided to compete and&nbsp;
eventually both made it to the District.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:12.560
I was able to come out on top and he&nbsp;
got second and ever since that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:12.560 --> 00:36:20.160
he has been right beside me, so…it was a-it&nbsp;
was a-an opportunity again to try something new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:27.360
which was this virtual platform. But yes, three&nbsp;
times before and this-this I guess is the charm.&nbsp;

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:31.680
-And every time you get better and better, right?&nbsp;

00:36:32.960 --> 00:36:35.920
-Feels very good, honestly.&nbsp;
Very, very good, thank you.&nbsp;

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:41.600
-All right, thanks. Verity, how&nbsp;
about you? Shall I read the—&nbsp;

00:36:41.600 --> 00:36:47.680
-First time, yeah? It's all good. First time was&nbsp;
2012 and I didn't prepare, and my sister beat me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.680 --> 00:36:52.160
at club, and that was when I discovered there&nbsp;
was the World Championship of Public Speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:59.440
and it opened my eyes to it. Second time was&nbsp;
2015 and I got as far as Division and came third.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:00.160 --> 00:37:04.960
And I-and the only reason I’m here today is&nbsp;
because of lockdown and because of COVID. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:04.960 --> 00:37:10.480
became a mother, I had left Toastmasters and if&nbsp;
we hadn't gone virtual and my club Toasted hadn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:16.560
needed some help with getting the club getting&nbsp;
going again 18 months ago, I probably wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.560 --> 00:37:21.360
have rejoined as a member yet. I was right in&nbsp;
the middle of motherhood, but suddenly we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:26.160
virtual, and I could do it because I could close&nbsp;
the door and I could practice around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:26.720 --> 00:37:30.160
and I’m just absolutely blown&nbsp;
away that I’ve ended up here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:38.240
today and I’m so grateful. So, so grateful.
-Sounds like you're-was your destiny. Nitay-Yair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:39.360 --> 00:37:44.240
how about you? Was this your first attempt&nbsp;
at the World Championship of Public Speaking?&nbsp;

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:50.000
-So, I in my first year at Toastmasters I&nbsp;
competed in the International Speech contest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:50.000 --> 00:37:53.920
and I’ve done a speech and one of my&nbsp;
mentors, Mike Keanu McCallum, said-I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.920 --> 00:37:57.600
mentioned that before, he said, “You need to&nbsp;
do the International Speech-” this is after my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:57.600 --> 00:38:01.520
icebreaker, “-you need to do the International&nbsp;
Speech Contest,” and I said, “Of course I do!”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:04.640
And then I went to the person next to me, I&nbsp;
said, “What's the International Speech Contest?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:11.920
I had no idea what it meant. So, I did I-did go&nbsp;
in that year, and with the help of Louise Beaton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:12.560 --> 00:38:17.600
I—who was a contestant in-she was competing&nbsp;
against me in a club, and she beat me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:17.600 --> 00:38:23.680
because I competed in two clubs—and she-she was&nbsp;
helping me and I was very lucky in my first year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:23.680 --> 00:38:28.800
extremely lucky and fortunate to win the District&nbsp;
and progress to the Quarterfinals. I did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:33.920
progress beyond the Quarterfinals. And I think&nbsp;
the first time I did it, it was-I-I still don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:39.280
know anything, I’m still learning, I always learn.&nbsp;
And-and I was still discovering the tools in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:43.360
thing that is called the art of public speaking.&nbsp;
So, I was patching myself as I was competing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:47.120
and-and I presume that's the&nbsp;
reason why I didn't progress beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:47.120 --> 00:38:52.480
the regionals. This year is my second year,&nbsp;
and-and I think the-the advantage as you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:52.480 --> 00:38:59.760
it over and over again, and I can't wait to&nbsp;
do it next year, is you-you don't learn...you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.760 --> 00:39:04.480
now know the tools and you now can experiment&nbsp;
with the tools, and you can become a true artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:04.480 --> 00:39:10.080
in in what you do. I’m not there, that-the-the&nbsp;
road is still long, but this is my second year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:15.200
and-and my second time I’m doing this, and-and&nbsp;
I just can't wait for next year. I really can't.&nbsp;

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:23.200
-Thank you. You-you seem like you're so full of&nbsp;
energy and-and ready to get started already on-on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.200 --> 00:39:29.440
the next speech for sure. Here's the next&nbsp;
question from our audience, and I’ll go to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:36.880
again first, Nitay-Yair, “What motivated&nbsp;
you to keep going after your first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:36.880 --> 00:39:41.840
embarrassing moment during&nbsp;
your Toastmasters journey?”&nbsp;

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:51.760
-Sorry, I’m not sure I-I understand the-the&nbsp;
question. So, what made me go forward after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:56.160
the embarrassing moment, my first embarrassing&nbsp;
moment at Toastmasters, is that right?&nbsp;

00:39:56.160 --> 00:40:00.240
-I-I believe the person—I don't want to&nbsp;
put words in their mouth, but they may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.240 --> 00:40:06.560
making the assumption that each of us have had an&nbsp;
embarrassing moment and we've pushed beyond that.&nbsp;

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:12.320
-I see. And they're not wrong, they're not wrong.&nbsp;
And I think especially when-when-especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:13.120 --> 00:40:18.320
when you experiment, and-and all of us visited&nbsp;
clubs obviously too, I didn't realize that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:24.320
concept of visiting clubs existed last year. It&nbsp;
was just me and some-and Louise, essentially.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:30.240
And-and this year when I visited clubs and I&nbsp;
tried, you know, the time travel and, oh, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:30.240 --> 00:40:36.960
bombed so many times! It was horrible. Oh, and you&nbsp;
just fail and fail and fail. But-but if you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:36.960 --> 00:40:42.640
in the mindset of ‘I have a message to deliver,&nbsp;
I’m experimenting,’ this is an experiment ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:43.280 --> 00:40:48.480
to try different tools, to push that message&nbsp;
forward. And some of them may not work and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:48.480 --> 00:40:56.480
okay, but you're experimenting. And then you&nbsp;
choose the right tools at competition, um, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:56.480 --> 00:41:01.840
what drives you forward. Because you understand&nbsp;
that the feedback you get is not to destroy you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:05.840
but actually to build you. And that's the&nbsp;
greatest thing about Toastmasters—the feedback.&nbsp;

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:12.560
-Thank you for that. Verity, what about&nbsp;
you? After your first embarrassing moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.240 --> 00:41:17.200
during your Toastmaster journey,&nbsp;
what helped you push through?&nbsp;

00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:25.760
-I would have to agree with Nitay-Yair, it's very&nbsp;
much in this contest journey, uh, before this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.480 --> 00:41:31.120
speeches were just part of the program, and I was&nbsp;
having fun at my club. But putting myself in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:36.640
spotlight as a contestant and receiving a lot of&nbsp;
feedback and sometimes having things go wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:36.640 --> 00:41:41.840
I’ve had some-some low moments over the last few&nbsp;
months and “why am I doing this” and “is it any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.840 --> 00:41:47.120
good” and then you come back to the message. And I&nbsp;
go, “I’m here because I have something to say. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.120 --> 00:41:52.160
here because I believe in it and the feedback&nbsp;
I’m getting is going to help it get better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.160 --> 00:41:57.200
So, I need to get over myself, I need to go back&nbsp;
to the drawing board, do some tweaks and then go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:57.200 --> 00:42:03.760
practice at a club and be okay with things not&nbsp;
being okay.” And so, for me it really is can I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.760 --> 00:42:08.880
be okay with things not being okay? And if&nbsp;
I need a bit of time to-to lick my wounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:09.920 --> 00:42:14.080
after a particular fall, that's all right. But&nbsp;
it's do I get back up and-and what makes me get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.080 --> 00:42:20.320
back up is the message if-each and every time.
-Oh, thank you for that beautiful share.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.040 --> 00:42:26.000
Roger, you look really serious there for a&nbsp;
moment, so I think you've got something you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:29.440
went to a moment in time, but what motivated you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:29.440 --> 00:42:36.160
to keep going after your first embarrassing&nbsp;
moment during your Toastmasters journey?&nbsp;

00:42:38.160 --> 00:42:44.960
-Well, mine would be again in a comp-in&nbsp;
competitive fashion. It was in 2015 and I shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.960 --> 00:42:51.760
this earlier at the other showcase. My grandmother&nbsp;
had passed away two days before I was heading to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:52.320 --> 00:42:58.560
Las Vegas for the World Championships, and I was&nbsp;
doing a speech about her. And while I was there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:04.560
for the first two days I was going through&nbsp;
an array of emotions, I was doubting myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:10.960
constantly, wondering why I was coming here to do&nbsp;
this speech when I was feeling the way I was about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:18.240
her passing away. And then five minutes before&nbsp;
going on stage I went right into a convenience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:26.320
store and bought myself a mirror as a prop. Now&nbsp;
everybody knows you don't practice a prop on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:33.120
greatest moment right there, that you should be&nbsp;
practicing this well before. And I chose to do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.120 --> 00:43:38.640
then and I went on stage, and I got to that part&nbsp;
where I pulled the mirror out of my pocket and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.280 --> 00:43:45.360
said my line, and then I went to put the mirror&nbsp;
back and it would not go back into the pocket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:46.240 --> 00:43:52.240
And I was sitting on stage trying to play&nbsp;
it off so much times and then it fell onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:52.240 --> 00:43:58.720
the ground. Um, I believe it was my grandmother&nbsp;
saying from the heavens above, “Why would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.720 --> 00:44:06.160
do that when you knew the speech well?” And I&nbsp;
remember I was so embarrassed from that moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:06.160 --> 00:44:12.960
but it was great people when I got back to my&nbsp;
District that constantly was picking me up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:13.760 --> 00:44:21.840
realizing how painful that moment was for me.&nbsp;
And even, again a good friend of mine, Neil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.400 --> 00:44:27.920
he would make jokes about it but I’m gonna go with&nbsp;
what Verity said. You have to be able to laugh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:27.920 --> 00:44:32.400
about it afterwards and that's what I was able&nbsp;
to do. It took about a year and a half before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:32.400 --> 00:44:37.280
I was able to just go, “Oh well, I dropped the&nbsp;
mirror,” and then I pushed forward and then I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:43.360
a speech about it—about dropping the mirror.&nbsp;
So, that was the easiest way to get over it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:43.360 --> 00:44:50.640
and that has-is what has propelled me to continue&nbsp;
forward and just deliver speeches and know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.640 --> 00:44:55.120
how those vulnerable moments can actually&nbsp;
help not only the audience but yourself.&nbsp;

00:44:57.120 --> 00:45:02.080
-Thank you for that share, particularly&nbsp;
about that we as Toastmasters, we may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:02.080 --> 00:45:07.360
not be wanting to compete ourselves but&nbsp;
what an influence that we have and what an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:07.360 --> 00:45:13.280
impact that we can have on those in our club&nbsp;
to keep going and to keep motivating. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:13.280 --> 00:45:20.400
keep on sharing those positive messages.&nbsp;
This next question is just directed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:20.960 --> 00:45:32.000
Nitay-Yair, and this is, “Where did you get&nbsp;
the idea of the black screen flashback?”&nbsp;

00:45:32.000 --> 00:45:37.840
-Okay, so the approach I took this year is to&nbsp;
focus on the art of public speaking and not on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:37.840 --> 00:45:42.960
public speaking, and I’m emphasizing&nbsp;
the art. And-and the way-the way-the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.400 --> 00:45:49.440
way I approached it is it-everything starts with a&nbsp;
message, so you have your well-crafted message and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:55.600
now you're looking for tools to push it forward,&nbsp;
right? And I thought okay because we're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:55.600 --> 00:46:03.280
about art, let's look outside of public speaking.&nbsp;
And there's different elements that happens there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:10.000
and-and to that particular thing, I took it from&nbsp;
three different art forms. One is beatboxing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:15.120
you know, because I was looking for sound for the&nbsp;
transfer. So, it's like-so that's a beatbox-sorry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:15.120 --> 00:46:20.560
I-I’ve found a sound that they used in movies,&nbsp;
but I couldn't do it, so I looked at beatboxing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:20.560 --> 00:46:26.800
how they-how they do the sound. That's how I&nbsp;
formulated the-the sound. The-the covering of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:26.800 --> 00:46:34.080
screen and opening, I took from TikTok—I-I'm not&nbsp;
a TikTok user, but I see-I see my little sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:34.080 --> 00:46:38.560
is. She's always on TikTok, she sends me these&nbsp;
videos and you know. But they edit the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:38.560 --> 00:46:43.360
they cover the-the screen and then when they open&nbsp;
it then on a beach. You know, they edit the video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:43.360 --> 00:46:48.800
I said, oh, that's a cool transition. So, that's&nbsp;
from Y generation or Z generation, I’m not, I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.800 --> 00:46:54.800
generation X. So, that's another art form, right,&nbsp;
of movies editing movies? But the issue I had is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:54.800 --> 00:47:01.520
okay, how do I go from very close to the screen&nbsp;
all the way to the back in a split second, yeah?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:01.520 --> 00:47:07.840
And that's from dance, so it's actually only two&nbsp;
steps. So, just this transition which looks very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:07.840 --> 00:47:15.360
simple has three different art forms in it. One is&nbsp;
from beatbox, how to do the sound. Two is from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.360 --> 00:47:20.400
transition from TikTok or from media, which I’m&nbsp;
not an expert in that. And three is from dance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:20.400 --> 00:47:26.720
how to do the two steps which appears very quickly&nbsp;
taking you to-to the other side of the screen. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:27.520 --> 00:47:32.880
that's how I did it. How did I come up with&nbsp;
it? Um, I think you just need to be creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:32.880 --> 00:47:38.160
and think, “Okay, I don't want to just step&nbsp;
back, let's spice it up a little bit.” And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:38.160 --> 00:47:42.400
by the way that wasn't always the transition, I&nbsp;
had different transitions that I experiment with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:42.400 --> 00:47:47.680
This was the one that best served the message&nbsp;
from the ones that I came up with. That's it.&nbsp;

00:47:49.040 --> 00:47:55.440
-Fascinating to bring in concepts&nbsp;
from other art forms and to utilize it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:55.440 --> 00:48:04.320
in this environment. Our next question will go&nbsp;
to Roger first, “What was your main inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:04.320 --> 00:48:11.040
for deciding to compete, and what were you&nbsp;
expecting to get from this whole journey?”&nbsp;

00:48:13.840 --> 00:48:21.600
-That's a good question. The inspiration&nbsp;
to compete was, again, Sanjay Sharma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:22.720 --> 00:48:27.920
I didn't come into Toastmasters to&nbsp;
compete, I just wanted to feel comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:28.720 --> 00:48:35.920
speaking in front of small crowds, in executive&nbsp;
office, in-in front of those types of situations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:36.640 --> 00:48:44.800
And it was him that kind of put that, you know,&nbsp;
that little fire. Set that fire off and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:44.800 --> 00:48:51.680
the inspiration that I needed, and then constantly&nbsp;
looking at so many members in my club at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:52.320 --> 00:48:58.640
Those who were in-were competing that were&nbsp;
much better than me, I would study them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:58.640 --> 00:49:05.200
look at them learn from them and then try to craft&nbsp;
my own style from that. And-what was your second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:05.200 --> 00:49:09.760
question? I’m sorry, the second part?
-It was, “What were you expecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:09.760 --> 00:49:17.920
to get from the whole journey?”
-Just to learn more about myself, to look at other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:18.560 --> 00:49:25.920
established and well-seasoned speakers. And&nbsp;
look, we have here individuals who are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:25.920 --> 00:49:32.000
new to Toastmasters and still made it here, and&nbsp;
they have so much to offer, even for me who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:36.400
been in Toastmasters for 10 years. I’m looking&nbsp;
at this and I am going to say it right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:36.400 --> 00:49:41.920
Nitay-Yair, I am jealous because when&nbsp;
you did that move, I was saying, “Whoa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:43.440 --> 00:49:47.200
I wish I could have done learned something&nbsp;
to do in my speech when I was saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:47.200 --> 00:49:51.040
you know, when you were-if you've been&nbsp;
blind by those who inspire you,” I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:51.040 --> 00:49:54.320
trying to figure out something else like,&nbsp;
“My hand doesn't look good there like that.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:54.880 --> 00:49:59.680
That was so cool, so I want to tell you&nbsp;
that right now publicly, so. But, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:59.680 --> 00:50:05.600
when I see that that's for me what it all comes&nbsp;
down to—developing my skills, learning how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.600 --> 00:50:11.840
become better and watching the very best and we&nbsp;
can learn from them, so that's what it is for me.&nbsp;

00:50:12.640 --> 00:50:18.480
-Beautiful. And I love that you shared that's&nbsp;
the accolades for each of-you know, you're all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:18.480 --> 00:50:23.680
in this together and you're just so supportive of&nbsp;
one another that-I’ve noticed that when we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:23.680 --> 00:50:30.000
preparing for the World Championship how&nbsp;
supportive you all are of each other and enjoying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:37.280
each other's placements. Well done. Verity, over&nbsp;
to you on that same question. What was your main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:37.280 --> 00:50:43.760
inspiration for deciding to compete? What were&nbsp;
you expecting to get from this whole journey?&nbsp;

00:50:46.080 --> 00:50:51.760
-My inspiration came last year when I wrote-I&nbsp;
started the pathways journey, and I did my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:51.760 --> 00:50:56.720
icebreaker, and I shared the story of walking on&nbsp;
the beach with my parents and being embarrassed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.720 --> 00:51:03.120
by them. And something in me, like this little&nbsp;
voice said, “This could be a contest speech.” And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:04.160 --> 00:51:09.200
probably two years earlier, I remember thinking&nbsp;
I’ve been lucky to win quite a few of the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:09.200 --> 00:51:12.480
contests at District level and I thought, “You&nbsp;
know what? The one thing I’m never going to win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:12.480 --> 00:51:17.520
is the International Speech Contest. I just don't&nbsp;
have a speech that works.” And suddenly this voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:17.520 --> 00:51:24.000
said, “I think the speech could go there,” and I&nbsp;
listened. I listened and it has changed so much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:27.520
my semi-final speech from what I delivered&nbsp;
at club level and all the way through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:28.560 --> 00:51:32.880
and-and with it came this dream of like&nbsp;
‘but-but what if I could go all the way?’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:33.600 --> 00:51:38.880
And it's been this incredible tug-of-war inside&nbsp;
of me and I try to explain it to someone, I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:38.880 --> 00:51:43.360
“This whole journey has felt like going on&nbsp;
your first date with someone and thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:43.360 --> 00:51:47.840
this is the person I’m going to marry and&nbsp;
having clarity that this is your future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:47.840 --> 00:51:50.640
you know, partner. And then you've still&nbsp;
got to date them, and you've still got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:50.640 --> 00:51:54.800
see if it's going to work out.” So, I’ve had&nbsp;
this tug-of-war, like maybe this could take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.800 --> 00:52:00.320
me all the way, but oh, I’m not sure, I’m&nbsp;
not sure. So, for me the journey was…I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:01.280 --> 00:52:08.160
never really pushed myself where I’ve had to give&nbsp;
it everything. I’ve always done enough to do well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:08.160 --> 00:52:14.800
in life, and this was deeply uncomfortable.&nbsp;
This was “sit down, write, rewrite, feedback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:14.800 --> 00:52:20.640
try again,” and-and I put myself in it&nbsp;
for that and I put my-my name in the hat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:26.480
and I owned that I wanted to go as far as I could.&nbsp;
And it was so scary to even say that out loud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:26.480 --> 00:52:31.120
And I’m so grateful I did, because it has,&nbsp;
it's-well, it's changed my life and I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:31.120 --> 00:52:35.120
it is going to continue to change my life. But&nbsp;
what I can say to people is when you have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:35.120 --> 00:52:39.520
little voice that says, ‘maybe this could go&nbsp;
somewhere,’ listen and see where it takes you.&nbsp;

00:52:41.360 --> 00:52:46.400
-That is-that's a good voice, isn't it? To hear&nbsp;
that we have that-those two sides that's saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:46.400 --> 00:52:51.920
‘You can do it,’ and that's saying, ‘Yeah, I&nbsp;
don't think so.’ And, uh, to go in between those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:51.920 --> 00:52:58.000
voices. That's the key, and you did it and pushed&nbsp;
yourself beyond, so bravo and we are so thankful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:58.000 --> 00:53:04.880
that you did. Nitay-Yair, what about the&nbsp;
same thing? What was your main inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:04.880 --> 00:53:13.840
for deciding to compete, and what were you&nbsp;
expecting to get from your whole journey?&nbsp;

00:53:14.560 --> 00:53:19.360
-So, I have to be-I’ll answer that in&nbsp;
two parts—actually, three parts. Uh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:19.360 --> 00:53:23.360
last year was my first year in Toastmasters, the&nbsp;
first year I’ve done the contest, I wanted to win.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:24.080 --> 00:53:29.040
And that was the wrong reason to go into this&nbsp;
competition, absolutely the wrong reason.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:29.920 --> 00:53:37.120
This year, I-I said, “Okay, obviously the semi's&nbsp;
final-the semi speech was the one that brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:37.120 --> 00:53:42.080
me to the finals,” and that message was very raw&nbsp;
and very important to me, “The Scars of the Past.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:42.080 --> 00:53:46.720
If you haven't watched it, the audience, please&nbsp;
do; it's a message that is very important to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:47.360 --> 00:53:54.640
And-and I decided to do against all the advice,&nbsp;
remove nearly all the humor in the compe-in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:54.640 --> 00:54:00.560
the speech, and that's the dedication that I&nbsp;
had to-to the message. I decided to focus on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:00.560 --> 00:54:05.760
message and whatever that means contest-wise, so&nbsp;
be it. And I looked at the contest as a platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:05.760 --> 00:54:11.280
to share that message with other people. And we&nbsp;
started in my club in my area, and then thankfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:11.280 --> 00:54:17.200
people appreciated the message, so I focused&nbsp;
on the message. But there's another component&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:17.200 --> 00:54:23.600
to it to-to-which is the semis. I won't go too&nbsp;
much into the details, but I nearly didn't-did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:23.600 --> 00:54:30.480
not compete in the semi-finals. I had some, uh,&nbsp;
issue and in 48 hours I was ready to pull out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.480 --> 00:54:36.320
the contest. And then what inspired me to do that,&nbsp;
and I’m getting emotional just thinking about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:36.320 --> 00:54:42.240
is LaShunda Rundles. Um, yeah, LaShunda Rundles.&nbsp;
I’ve never met her, I-I’ve only seen her speeches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:42.240 --> 00:54:47.280
And I thought if she could do it, if she could&nbsp;
deliver a speech—and I’m getting emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:47.280 --> 00:54:53.360
thinking about it—I have no reason why I can't do&nbsp;
it. There's no reason. So, if you believe in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:53.360 --> 00:54:59.440
message you're just gonna do it. That's-that's&nbsp;
the inspiration that you need to have. And I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:59.440 --> 00:55:06.160
very thankful that she existed and-and gave me&nbsp;
that inspiration to do the semis, simple as that.&nbsp;

00:55:06.160 --> 00:55:11.680
-Well thank you for mentioning her, she, was&nbsp;
a remarkable person. Absolutely, for sure. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:11.680 --> 00:55:20.000
that wisdom about don't focus on the win, focus on&nbsp;
the message. Focus on the message. Thank you. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:31.200
we have time for one more, and the question was&nbsp;
“How did you,” …oh, this one is the last question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:32.240 --> 00:55:39.280
and it's a good one: “What will you do next?” And&nbsp;
we'll go with Roger first and then Nitay and over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:39.280 --> 00:55:45.240
to Verity. So, Roger, what will you do next?
-I’m going to go to Disney World. [Laughter]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.520 --> 00:55:52.640
I really wanted to say that, but… you know&nbsp;
what? I’m going to take some time. I’m going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:52.640 --> 00:55:59.280
to take some time off; I’ve been competing for the&nbsp;
majority of my Toastmasters journey; I’m going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:59.280 --> 00:56:06.400
take some time to myself and do what I do-what I&nbsp;
love doing the most and that is helping others to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:07.120 --> 00:56:13.200
finally achieve their goals and their dreams. I&nbsp;
know that there are so many people in District 86&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:13.200 --> 00:56:24.080
and now in District 123 that I can help and&nbsp;
get them to see what they can possibly do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:24.640 --> 00:56:31.200
what dreams they may have. So, I’m gonna focus&nbsp;
on that for now, take some time to just relax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:31.200 --> 00:56:38.720
and…who knows? You never know. Maybe I might&nbsp;
come back, maybe I won't. for me right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:38.720 --> 00:56:46.400
it's about seeing the rest of my District&nbsp;
that have been so supportive of me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:47.200 --> 00:56:52.080
that I try to help them and the great speakers&nbsp;
that we have in both those Districts now.&nbsp;

00:56:52.720 --> 00:56:57.840
-Oh, thank you for that.&nbsp;
Nitay-Yair, what will you do next?&nbsp;

00:56:59.600 --> 00:57:04.640
-In line for Toastmasters? That's-that's two&nbsp;
separate questions. Uh, I was very fortunate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:04.640 --> 00:57:09.520
in the last two days to spend time with the&nbsp;
family, and that's something that obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:09.520 --> 00:57:15.200
my incredible wife sacrificed in support, so I’m&nbsp;
very, very thankful for her support, otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:15.200 --> 00:57:23.280
I wouldn't be-I wouldn't be here. In terms of the&nbsp;
Toastmasters journey, my heart is already hurting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:23.280 --> 00:57:28.800
and my brain is already exploding because&nbsp;
there's so much-so many messages I want to-I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:28.800 --> 00:57:33.520
want to bring out. So, this morning I already&nbsp;
started writing the speech for-for next year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:34.480 --> 00:57:39.360
And I’m actually not joking; I’m fully serious.&nbsp;
And the reason being this is the greatest thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:39.360 --> 00:57:44.480
about Toastmasters, because I was very shy for&nbsp;
a long time, and-and you have a lot of desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:44.480 --> 00:57:49.520
inside but when it's all locked and you're unable&nbsp;
to express it and then this comes, this amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:49.520 --> 00:57:54.880
platform and that teaches you all the tools and&nbsp;
craft how to express it…I mean God bless, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:54.880 --> 00:58:01.760
a blessing! How can you not want to do it? Yeah?
-Well thank you for that, we're so grateful that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.760 --> 00:58:09.040
“Brown Eyes” has allowed you to have some more&nbsp;
time with us and that she's letting you continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:09.040 --> 00:58:14.960
to do what you've come to love, absolutely.&nbsp;
All right, Verity, what will you do next?&nbsp;

00:58:17.520 --> 00:58:22.560
-Oh, other than spend some much-needed time&nbsp;
with my family who have been missing me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:22.560 --> 00:58:27.600
I’ve got a lot of thank-you letters to&nbsp;
write to everyone who supported me. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:28.160 --> 00:58:34.800
taking some time back and then in terms of my&nbsp;
work and what I’m passionate about, my final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:34.800 --> 00:58:40.480
speech has given me a whole new direction and I’m&nbsp;
very inspired to start working on a program that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:40.480 --> 00:58:44.480
whether I’m offering it into my clients&nbsp;
or I’m not sure yet it's-it's percolating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:44.480 --> 00:58:50.240
but how to help people write a different story,&nbsp;
and what's really exciting is it actually brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:50.240 --> 00:58:56.960
my music back to life. I walked away from my&nbsp;
professional music career 10-10-11 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:56.960 --> 00:59:01.520
and suddenly I’m seeing that my songs&nbsp;
and the speech, there's something there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:01.520 --> 00:59:06.720
So, I’m just saying that out loud because that's&nbsp;
what's here right now. I feel like this is my next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:06.720 --> 00:59:11.840
bit of work. And, uh, in terms of Toastmasters…&nbsp;
[Clears throat] Excuse me, too much speaking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:12.400 --> 00:59:18.240
In terms of Toastmasters, it's how do I bring back&nbsp;
what I’ve learned to my District, like Roger was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:18.240 --> 00:59:25.040
saying. And I’m just so excited to help the-the&nbsp;
other incredible speakers in Southern Africa get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:25.040 --> 00:59:30.160
the opportunity to hopefully get to where I’m&nbsp;
sitting today, so that's-that's my commitment.&nbsp;

00:59:31.840 --> 00:59:36.480
-Outstanding. Thank you for sharing,&nbsp;
we look forward to seeing that, uh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:36.480 --> 00:59:42.800
evolve and how you help others write a different&nbsp;
story, those that are in need. Thank you so much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:43.440 --> 00:59:52.240
And thank you and congratulations again to each&nbsp;
of our 2021 World Championship Winners. You have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:52.240 --> 01:00:00.880
reached this goal after many months of hard work,&nbsp;
practice and refinement of your speaking skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:00.880 --> 01:00:09.200
or the speaking art. Enjoy this moment, because&nbsp;
you have definitely earned it. Members and guests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:09.200 --> 01:00:25.040
thank you for attending this showcase, it's been&nbsp;
a pleasure to have each of you here with us today.

01:00:25.040 --> 01:00:25.540
[Applause]

